B.QB=new A.bC(B.wC,["The Air Force has the most completely public UAS stack of any service, posted on AF e-Publishing with no releasability restrictions.\n\n\u2022 AFPD 11-5 \u2014 the sUAS policy directive at the top of the chain.\n\u2022 DAFMAN 11-501 \u2014 Small UAS Management: the approval chain (AF/A3S \u2192 MAJCOMs \u2192 installation commanders \u2192 squadron commanders \u2192 unit sUAS program managers), airworthiness and authority-to-operate, airspace coordination, and the DAF COA request checklist. It also carries the DoD UAS Categories (Groups 1\u20135) as Attachment 2.\n\u2022 DAFMAN 11-502 \u2014 Group 1 and 2 sUAS operations: training, standardization, and operating procedures.\n\u2022 AFMAN 11-202 Vol 3 \u2014 Flight Operations, which governs Group 4\u20135 UAS.\n\nCorpus ingest from these public PDFs is the next step.","Army UAS governance is folded into the Army's main aviation regulation rather than a drone-only rulebook.\n\n\u2022 AR 95-1 \u2014 Flight Regulations: the single-source flight regulation for manned and unmanned aviation, including UAS airspace-integration policy and small-UAS guidance in Appendix D. It superseded the old UAS-specific AR 95-23.\n\u2022 Training circulars (TC series) \u2014 the UAS and small-UAS commander/aircrew training programs.\n\nPublic via the Army Publishing Directorate. Corpus ingest is the next step.","The Navy has no public, UAS-specific operations instruction like the Air Force's 11-50x series. Navy UAS governance sits inside two general frameworks.\n\n\u2022 OPNAVINST 3710.7 \u2014 NATOPS General Flight and Operating Instructions: the umbrella under which naval UAS operations and flight clearances are framed.\n\u2022 NAVAIRINST 13034.1 \u2014 the flight-clearance authority: every Department of the Navy unmanned system requires a NAVAIR flight clearance before flight.\n\nThe detailed model-specific NATOPS manuals are not publicly posted \u2014 in this app they stay titled placeholders, never summarized from guesswork.","Marine Corps sUAS governance runs through training-and-readiness standards and fast-moving administrative messages.\n\n\u2022 NAVMC 3500.107 \u2014 Group 1 and 2 UAS training-and-readiness standards, the core USMC UAS publication against which operator certifications are issued.\n\u2022 Current sUAS training and certification requirements issued under the Secretary's 2025 drone-dominance guidance, with MARADMINs (e.g. 537/25, 624/25) as the fastest-moving public channel.\n\nPublic on marines.mil. Corpus ingest is the next step."],t.li)
